Implemented `variable_length_quantity.py` with big-endian 7-bit-group VLQ:

- `encode(numbers)` — splits each 32-bit int into 7-bit chunks (LSB-first, then reversed), sets continuation bit `0x80` on every byte except the last, and concatenates across the input list.
- `decode(bytes_)` — accumulates `(value << 7) | (byte & 0x7F)`, emits a value whenever bit 7 is clear, and raises `ValueError("incomplete byte sequence")` if the stream ends mid-value.

Verified by running the code (no test files created): `python3 -m py_compile` passed, and a temporary out-of-repo script (in `/var/folders/.../opencode/`) exercised all 18 encode vectors and 6 decode vectors from the canonical Exercism suite, the 3 incomplete-sequence error cases (`[0xFF]`, `[0x80]`, `[0xC0]` all raise non-empty `ValueError`), plus 10,000 random full-range round-trips — result: `FAILS: 0 / PASS`.

Note: I did not create `tests/` workflow artifacts because the task explicitly forbids creating test files; the workspace had no existing scripts/ or test suite.
